{
  "id": "COMPOUND:30160 - PROTEIN:3153",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a complex but interconnected pathway linking the compound to the Formaldehyde-activating enzyme. The compound is identified as a target of two distinct drugs, K-252a and 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline, both of which are targets of the Dual specificity protein kinase CLK2. This kinase, in turn, is a target of K-252a, which directly interacts with the Formaldehyde-activating enzyme. The evidence indicates that the compound may act through the CLK2 kinase, which is a key player in the activation of formaldehyde. The presence of multiple pathways converging on the Formaldehyde-activating enzyme suggests that the compound may influence the enzyme through a kinase-dependent mechanism, possibly by modulating the activity of CLK2. This mechanism is supported by the shared target of K-252a and CLK2, and the direct interaction between K-252a and the enzyme. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential role of the compound in regulating formaldehyde metabolism through a kinase-mediated pathway.",
